The Greatest in the Kingdom of Heaven
At that hour the disciples came to Jesus, saying, "Who then is greatest in the kingdom of heaven?"
Then Jesus called a child to Him and set him in their midst.
And he said, "Truly I tell you, unless you turn back and become like children, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever places himself like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
And before, he accepted only one son like this in my name.
And whoever causes one of these little believers to stumble, it would be better for him to have a millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.
Woe to the world because of the stumbling blocks! Surely stumbling blocks must come, but woe to that person through whom the stumbling block comes!
If your hand or your foot causes you to stumble, cut it off and cast it away from you. It is better for you to enter life lame or maimed than to be thrown into the eternal fire with two hands or two feet.
And if your eye causes you to stumble, pluck it out and cast it away from you. It is better for you to enter life with one eye than to be thrown into the fire of Hell with two eyes. Like the lost sheep.
"See that you do not despise one of these little ones, for I tell you that their angels in heaven always see the face of my Father who is in heaven."
For the Son of Man came to save what was lost.
What do you think? If a man has a hundred sheep, and one of them goes astray, will he not leave the ninety-nine on the mountains and go to look for the lost one?
And if it happens that he finds it, truly I tell you: he rejoices over it more than over the ninety-nine that did not go astray.
This is not the will of your Father in heaven that one of these little ones should perish. If your brother sins against you
If your brother sins against you, go and rebuke him privately between you two alone. If he listens to you, you have gained your brother.
And if he does not listen, take with you one or two others, so that every matter may be established by the testimony of two or three witnesses.
And if they do not listen to you, tell it to the church. And if they do not listen even to the church, let it be to you as the Gentile and the tax collector.
Truly I tell you: Whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
And I tell you also: If two of you agree on earth about anything they ask for, it will be done for them by my Father who is in heaven,
"For where two or three gather in my name, there am I with them." Like the servant who does not forgive
Then Peter came to him and said, "Lord, how many times shall I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus said to him, "I do not say to you seven times, but seventy times seven times."
Therefore, the kingdom of heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.
When he began the accounting, one debtor owed him ten thousand dinars.
And since he did not have what would fulfill his master's order to sell himself, his wife, his children, and all that he had, and to pay the debt.
The servant's pride bowed down to him, saying: O master, be patient with me, and I will repay you in full.
The master showed mercy to that servant and set him free, and forgave his debt.
When that servant went out, he found one of his fellow servants who owed him a hundred dinars, so he seized him and began to choke him, saying: Pay me what you owe.
The servant's pride was his companion on his feet, and he asked him, saying: Wait for me, and I will repay you all.
He did not refuse but went and threw him into prison until the debt was paid.
When the slaves saw what had happened to their companions, they were very sad. They came and told their master everything that had occurred.
Then his master called him and said to him: O wicked servant, all that debt I left to you because you asked me.
Shouldn't you also have mercy on your companion servant just as I have mercy on you?
And his master was angry and handed him over to the tormentors until he should pay back all that was owed to him.
Thus does the Heavenly Father deal with you if you do not forgive each other's faults from your hearts.
Marriage and Divorce
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went to the region of Judea beyond the Jordan.
And many crowds followed him, and he healed them there.
Then the Pharisees came to him to test him, saying, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any reason?"
He answered and said to them, "Have you not read that He who created from the beginning created them male and female?"
And he said: For this reason a man shall leave his father and his mother and be joined to his wife, and the two shall become one flesh.
So they are no longer two, but one body. Therefore what God has joined together, let no one separate.
They said to him: "Then why did Moses recommend that a book of divorce be given so that she may be divorced?"
He said to them: "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because of the hardness of your hearts. But it was not like this from the beginning.
And I say to you: Whoever divorces his wife except for the reason of adultery and marries another commits adultery, and he who marries a divorced woman commits adultery.
His disciples said to him, "If this is how a man should be with a woman, it is better not to marry!"
So he said to them: "Not everyone accepts this word, but only those to whom it has been given,
For there are eunuchs who were born that way from their mothers' wombs, and there are eunuchs who were made eunuchs by men, and there are eunuchs who made themselves eunuchs for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. Let anyone who can accept this accept it." Jesus blesses the children
Then the children were brought to him that he might lay his hands on them and pray, but the disciples rebuked them.
But Jesus said, "Let the children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
He placed his hands on them and went from there. The rich young man
And someone came forward and said to him, "Good Teacher, what good deed must I do to have eternal life?"
So he said to him: "Why do you call me good? No one is good except one, and that is God. But if you want to enter life, keep the commandments."
He said to him: "Which commandments?" Jesus said: "Do not kill. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not bear false witness."
Honor your father and your mother, and love your relative as yourself.
The young man said to him: "I have memorized all of this since my youth. So what else do I need?"
Jesus said to him, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven; then come, follow me."
When the young man heard the word, he went away sorrowful, because he had great wealth.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "Truly I tell you, it is hard for a rich man to enter the kingdom of heaven!"
And I tell you also: It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God!
When his disciples heard this, they were greatly astonished and said, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "With man this is impossible, but with God all things are possible."
Then Peter answered him, "See, we have left everything and followed you. What then will we have?"
Then Jesus said to them, "Truly I tell you, you who have followed me, when the Son of Man sits on his glorious throne, you also will sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.
And everyone who has left houses or brothers or sisters or father or mother or wife or children or fields for my name's sake will receive a hundredfold and inherit eternal life.
But many who are first will be last, and others who are last will be first.
Like the deed in generosity
For the kingdom of heaven is like a man, a householder, who went out early in the morning to hire laborers for his vineyard,
So he agreed with the worker on a dinar for the day, and sent them to his vineyard.
Then he went out around the third hour and saw others standing idle in the marketplace,
He said to them, "Go also to the vineyard, and whatever is right, I will give you." So they went.
He also went out around the sixth and ninth hour and did the same.
Then around the eleventh hour he went out and found others standing idle, and said to them: Why have you stood here all day idle?
They said to him: Because no one hired us. He said to them: You also go to the vineyard and take what is rightfully yours.
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard said to his agent: Call the workers and give them their wages, beginning with the last to the first.
Then came the people of the eleventh hour and took a denarius each.
When the first came, they thought they would receive more. So they also took a denarius each.
And while they were taking it, they complained about the Lord of the house.
Saying: These others worked for one hour, and you have made them equal to us who have borne the burden of the day and the heat!
He answered and said to one of them: O friend, I have not wronged you! Did we not agree on a dinar?
Take what is yours and go, for I want to give this last one to you as well.
Or is it not lawful for me to do what I want with what is mine? Or is your eye evil because I am good?
Thus the last will be first, and the first will be last, because many are called, but few are chosen." Jesus foretells his death and resurrection.
And as Jesus was going up to Jerusalem, he took the twelve disciples aside on the way and said to them:
Here we are going up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be delivered to the chief priests and scribes, and they will condemn him to death,
And they will hand him over to the Gentiles to be mocked, flogged, and crucified; and on the third day he will rise.” The mother of Zebedee’s sons asked.
Then the mother of the sons of Zebedee came to him with her sons, and she bowed down and asked him for something.
He said to her, "What do you want?" She said to him, "Say that these two sons of mine may sit, one at your right and the other at your left, in your kingdom."
Jesus answered, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup I am going to drink, and be baptized with the baptism I am baptized with?" They said to him, "We can."
So he said to them, "As for my cup, you shall drink it, and with the dye with which I am dyed, you shall be dyed. But as for sitting at my right hand and at my left, it is not mine to give, but for those for whom it has been prepared by my Father."
When the ten heard, they were angry because of the two brothers.
Then Jesus called them and said, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them.
It shall not be so among you. But whoever wants to be great among you must be your servant,
And whoever wants to be first among you must be your servant,
"The Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many." Healing of two blind men in Jericho
And as they were leaving Jericho, a large crowd followed him,
And two blind men were sitting by the road. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, saying, "Have mercy on us, Lord, Son of David!"
So the crowd scolded them to be silent, but they cried out even more, saying: "Have mercy on us, O Lord, O son of David!"
Jesus stopped and called them, saying, "What do you want me to do for you?"
They said to him: "Lord, that our eyes may be opened!"
Jesus had compassion and touched their eyes, and immediately their eyes were opened, and they followed him.
Entry to Jerusalem
And when they drew near to Jerusalem and came to Bethphage at the Mount of Olives, then Jesus sent two disciples
He said to them: "Go to the village in front of you; immediately you will find a donkey tied and a colt with her. Untie them and bring them to me."
And if anyone says to you, say: The Lord needs them. At once he will send them.
All of this was so that what was said by the Prophet, the one who said:
Say to the daughter of Zion: Behold, your king is coming to you, gentle, riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey.
So the two disciples went and did as Jesus had instructed them,
And they brought the donkey and the colt, and they laid their garments on them, and he sat upon them.
And the majority spread their clothes on the road. Others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road.
And the crowds who went before and those who followed were shouting, saying: "Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest!"
And when he entered Jerusalem, the whole city was shaken, saying, "Who is this?"
And the crowds said, "This is Jesus the prophet from Nazareth of Galilee." Cleansing of the temple
And Jesus entered the temple of God and drove out all those who were buying and selling in the temple, and overturned the tables of the money changers and the seats of those who sold doves.
And he said to them: "It is written: My house shall be called a house of prayer. But you have made it a den of robbers!"
And my uncle approached him and limped into the temple, and he healed them.
When the chief priests and the scribes saw the wonderful things he did, and the children crying out in the temple, saying, "Hosanna to the Son of David!", they became angry.
And they said to him, "Do you hear what these are saying?" Jesus answered them, "Yes! Have you never read: Out of the mouths of infants and nursing babies you have prepared praise?"
Then he left them and went out of the city to Bethany and stayed there. The fig tree withers.
And in the morning, as he was returning to the city, he became hungry,
He looked at a fig tree by the road, and came to it but found nothing on it except leaves only. So he said to it, "May you never bear fruit again forever!" Immediately, the fig tree withered.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed and said, "How did the fig tree wither so quickly?"
Jesus answered them, "Truly I tell you, if you have faith and do not doubt, not only can you do what was done to the fig tree, but also if you say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' it will be done.
"And whatever you ask for in prayer, believing, you will receive." The question about the authority of Jesus
And when he came to the temple, the chief priests and the elders of the people approached him while he was teaching, saying, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
And Jesus answered and said to them, "I also will ask you one thing; if you tell me about it, I too will tell you by what authority I do these things:"
John's baptism: From where did it come? From heaven or from men?" And they reasoned with themselves, saying, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will ask, 'Then why did you not believe him?'"
And if we say: from the people, we fear the crowd, because John is like a prophet to everyone.
They answered Jesus, "We do not know." He also said to them, "Neither do I tell you by what authority I do these things. Like the two sons
What do you think? A man had a son, so he came to the first and said: O my son, go today work in my vineyard.
He answered and said: I do not want. But he finally regretted it and left.
He came to the second and said the same. So he answered and said: Here I am, my lord. And he did not go.
So which of the two did the will of the father?» They said to him: «The first.» Jesus said to them: «Truly I tell you, the tax collectors and the prostitutes are entering the kingdom of God ahead of you,
Because John came to you in the way of truth, but you did not believe in him; yet the tax collectors and the prostitutes believed in him. And when you saw this, you did not repent afterward to believe in him. The parable of the two sons.
Listen to another example: There was a man who was the head of a household; he planted a vineyard, fenced it in, dug a winepress in it, built a tower, handed it over to tenants, and went on a journey.
And when the time of the fruits drew near, he sent his servants to the tenants to collect his fruits.
So the noble ones took their slaves, flogged some, killed some, and stoned some.
Then he sent other servants, more than the first ones, and they did the same to them.
Finally, he sent them his son saying: They fear my son!
As for the noble ones, when they saw the son, they said among themselves: This is the heir! Come, let us kill him and take his inheritance!
So they seized him, led him out of the vineyard, and killed him.
So when the owner of generosity comes, what will he do with those generous ones?».
They said to him: "Those wicked ones will perish a terrible destruction, and the vineyard will be given to other noble ones who will give its fruits in their seasons."
Jesus said to them, "Have you never read in the Scriptures: The stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone? This was the Lord’s doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes!"
Therefore I tell you: The kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a people who will produce its fruits.
Whoever falls on this stone will groan in pain, and whoever falls on it will be crushed by it!».
And when the chief priests and the Pharisees heard his parables, they knew that he was speaking about them.
And when they sought to seize him, they feared the crowds, because he was regarded by them as a prophet.
Like the wedding of the king's son
And Jesus spoke to them also in parables, saying:
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who prepared a wedding banquet for his son,
And he sent his servants to call those who were invited to the wedding, but they did not want to come.
He also sent other servants, saying: Tell those who are invited, "Behold, I have prepared my dinner. My oxen and fattened cattle have been slaughtered, and everything is ready. Come to the wedding!"
But they were negligent and went on, one to his field, and another to his trade,
And the rest seized their slaves, insulted them, and killed them.
When the king heard, he became angry, sent his soldiers, destroyed those killers, and burned their city.
Then he said to his servants: As for the wedding feast, it is ready, but those who were invited were not worthy.
So go to the roads and country lanes and compel them to come in to the wedding feast.
So those servants went out into the roads and gathered all whom they found, both bad and good. And the wedding hall was filled with guests.
When the king entered to look at those reclining, he saw there a man who was not wearing wedding clothes.
He said to him: O companion, how did you enter here without wearing the wedding garment? He was silent.
Then the king said to the servants: Bind his feet and hands, take him and throw him into the outer darkness. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
"For many are called, but few are chosen." Paying the tribute to Caesar
Then the Pharisees went and conferred together on how to trap him with a word.
Then they sent to him their disciples along with the Herodians, saying, "Teacher, we know that you are truthful and teach the way of God in truth, and you show no partiality to anyone, for you do not regard the faces of men.
So tell us: what do you think? Is it permissible to pay tribute to Caesar or not?
Jesus, knowing their wickedness, said, "Why do you test me, you hypocrites?"
"Show me the jizya payment." So they brought him a dinar.
So he said to them, "Whose picture and writing is this?"
They said to him: "To Caesar." He said to them: "Give to Caesar what is Caesar's and to God what is God's."
When they heard this, they were amazed, left him, and went away. The question about the resurrection of the dead
On that day, Sadducees, who say there is no resurrection, came to him and asked him.
Saying: "O teacher, Moses said: If someone dies without children, his brother shall marry his wife and raise offspring for his brother."
We had seven brothers, and the first one married and died. And since he had no offspring, he left his wife to his brother.
And likewise the second and the third up to the seventh.
And the last of all, the woman also died.
On the Day of Resurrection, whose wife will she be among the seven? For she was for all of them!».
Jesus answered them, "You are mistaken because you do not know the Scriptures nor the power of God."
For in the resurrection they neither marry nor are given in marriage, but are like the angels of God in heaven.
As for the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what was said to you before by God, who said:
I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob? God is not the God of the dead but of the living.
When the crowds heard this, they were amazed at his teaching. The greatest commandment
As for the Pharisees, when they heard that he was mute, the Sadducees gathered together,
And one of them, who was my mosquito, asked him to test him, saying:
"Teacher, which commandment in the law is the greatest?"
And Jesus said to him, "You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind."
This is the first and greatest commandment.
And the second is like it: Love your neighbor as yourself.
"These two commandments encompass the whole law and the prophets." Christ and David
While the Pharisees were gathered, Jesus asked them.
Saying: "What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he?" They said to him: "Son of David."
He said to them: "How then does David call him Lord by the Spirit, saying:
The Lord said to my Lord: Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet.
If David calls him Lord, how then can he be his son?
No one was able to answer him with a word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him at all.
Warning against the scribes and the Pharisees
Then Jesus addressed the crowds and his disciples.
Saying: "On Moses' seat sat the scribes and the Pharisees,
So whatever they tell you to keep, keep it and do it, but do not act according to their deeds, because they say but do not do.
They load heavy, difficult burdens onto people's shoulders, but they themselves are unwilling to lift a finger to move them.
And all their deeds they do to be seen by people: so they widen their tassels and enlarge the fringes of their garments,
And they love the first resting place in the first gatherings, and the first seats in the assemblies,
And greetings in the markets, and that people call out to them: My master, my master!
But you, do not call anyone your master, for you have one teacher, the Christ, and you are all brothers.
And do not call anyone on earth your father, for your Father is one who is in heaven.
And do not call anyone teacher, for your teacher is one—the Christ.
And the greatest among you shall be your servant.
Whoever exalts himself will be humbled, and whoever humbles himself will be exalted. Woe to the scribes and the Pharisees.
But woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you shut the kingdom of heaven in people's faces; you neither enter yourselves nor allow those who want to enter to go in.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you devour widows' houses, and for a pretense make long prayers. Therefore you will receive greater condemnation.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you travel over land and sea to win a single proselyte, and when he is won, you make him twice as much a son of hell as yourselves.
Woe to you, blind leaders! Those who say: Whoever swears by the Temple is nothing, but whoever swears by the gold of the Temple is bound.
O ignorant and blind ones! Which is greater: the gold or the temple that sanctifies the gold?
Whoever swears by the altar, it is nothing; but whoever swears by the offering on it is bound to fulfill it.
O ignorant and blind ones! Which is greater: the offering or the altar that sanctifies the offering?
Whoever swears by the altar has sworn by it and by all that is upon it!
Whoever swears by the temple has sworn by it and by the one who dwells in it,
And whoever swears by the sky has sworn by the Throne of Allah and by the One who is seated upon it.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you tithe mint and dill and cumin, and have neglected the weightier matters of the law: justice and mercy and faithfulness. You ought to have done these things, without leaving the others undone.
O blind leaders! Who strain out a gnat and swallow a camel.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you cleanse the outside of the cup and the plate, but inside they are full of extortion and self-indulgence.
You blind Pharisee! First cleanse the inside of the cup and the plate, so that their outside may also be clean.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs, which outwardly appear beautiful, but within are full of dead bones and all uncleanness.
This is how you also are: outwardly you appear to people as righteous, but inwardly you are full of hypocrisy and wickedness.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you build the tombs of the prophets and decorate the graves of the righteous,
And you say: If we had been in the days of our fathers, we would not have shared in the blood of the prophets.
You testify against yourselves that you are the sons of the killers of the prophets.
So fill the measure of your forefathers.
O snakes' children! How do you flee from the judgment of Hell?
Therefore, I send to you prophets, wise men, and scribes; some of them you kill and crucify, others you flog in your synagogues and persecute from city to city,
That upon you may come all the innocent blood shed on the earth, from the blood of Abel the righteous to the blood of Zechariah son of Berechiah, whom you murdered between the temple and the altar.
Truly I tell you: all this will come upon this generation! Jesus laments over Jerusalem
"O Jerusalem, O Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you, how often I have desired to gather your children together, as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you were not willing!"
Behold, your house is left to you desolate.
For I tell you: you will not see me from now on until you say: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!
Signs of the End Times
Then Jesus went out and departed from the temple, and his disciples came forward to show him the buildings of the temple.
And Jesus said to them, "Do you not see all these? Truly I tell you, not one stone here will be left on another; every one will be thrown down!"
And while he was sitting on the Mount of Olives, the disciples came to him privately, saying: "Tell us, when will this be? And what will be the sign of your coming and the end of the age?"
And Jesus answered them, saying: "See to it that no one deceives you."
For many will come in my name, saying, "I am the Christ," and will deceive many.
And you will hear of wars and news of wars. Look, do not be alarmed. For all this must happen, but it is not yet the end.
For a nation rises upon a nation, and a kingdom upon a kingdom, and there will be famines, plagues, and earthquakes in various places.
But these are all the beginning of the pains.
Then they will hand you over to be persecuted and killed, and you will be hated by all nations because of my name.
And then many will stumble and betray one another, and will hate one another.
Many false prophets arise and lead many astray.
And due to the abundance of sin, the love of many grows cold.
But he who is patient to the end, he will be saved.
And he proclaims the good news of the kingdom throughout the whole world as a testimony to all nations. Then comes the end.
So when you see the "abomination of desolation" spoken of by the prophet Daniel standing in the holy place—let the reader understand—
Then those who are in Judea will flee to the mountains,
And the one on the roof does not come down to take anything from his house,
And the one in the field, let him not turn back to take his clothes.
Woe to the pregnant and nursing women in those days!
Arrive so that your escape is neither in winter nor on a Saturday,
For it will then be a great distress unlike any since the beginning of the world until now, nor will there be any.
And if those days were not shortened, no flesh would be saved. But for the sake of the elect, those days are shortened.
Then if anyone says to you, "Look, here is the Messiah!" or "There he is!" do not believe it.
For false messiahs and false prophets will arise and show great signs and wonders, so as to deceive, if possible, even the elect.
Here I have already preceded and informed you.
If they say to you: Here he is in the wilderness! Do not go out. Here he is in the inner rooms! Do not believe it.
For as the lightning comes from the east and shines as far as the west, so also will the coming of the Son of Man be.
For where the corpse is, there the vultures will gather.
And after the hardship of those days, the sun darkened, the moon gave no light, the stars fell from the sky, and the powers of the heavens were shaken.
And then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in the sky. And then all the tribes of the earth will mourn, and they will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of the sky with great power and glory.
So He will send His angels with a great trumpet blast, and they will gather His chosen ones from the four winds, from the ends of the heavens to their ends.
From the fig tree learn the lesson: when its branch becomes tender and puts forth leaves, you know that summer is near.
So also you, when you see all these things, know that it is near, at the doors.
Truly I tell you: this generation will not pass away until all these things have happened.
Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will never pass away. Always be prepared.
As for that Day and that Hour, no one knows about them, not even the angels of the heavens, except my Father alone.
Just as the days of Noah were, so will also be the coming of the Son of Man.
For as they were in the days before the flood, eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the day Noah entered the ark,
And they did not know until the flood came and took them all; so will the coming of the Son of Man be.
Then there will be two in the field; one will be taken and the other left.
Two grind at the millstone; one is taken and the other is left.
Stay awake, then, for you do not know at what hour your Lord will come.
And know this: if the Lord of the house knew at what time the thief would come, He would stay awake and not let His house be broken into.
Therefore, be ready yourselves also, for the Son of Man comes at an hour you do not expect. Like the faithful servant
So who is the faithful, wise servant whom his master put in charge of his servants to give them their food at the proper time?
Blessed is that servant whom his master finds doing so when he comes!
Truly I tell you: He will stake it all on his entire wealth.
But if that vile servant says in his heart: My master is delaying his coming.
He begins to beat the slaves, his companions, and eats and drinks with the drunkards.
The master of that servant comes on a day he does not expect and at an hour he does not know,
So He cuts it off and assigns its share with the hypocrites. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
Like the ten virgins
Then the kingdom of heaven will be like ten virgins who took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were wise, and five were ignorant.
As for the foolish ones, they took their lamps but did not take any oil with them,
As for the wise women, they took oil in their vessels with their lamps.
While the groom delayed, all of them became sleepy and drowsy.
In the middle of the night there was a cry: Behold, the bridegroom is coming; go out to meet him!
Then all those virgins arose and trimmed their lamps.
So the ignorant women said to the wise women: Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are going out.
So the wise women answered, saying: It may not be enough for us, but go to the sellers and buy for yourselves.
And while they were going to buy, the bridegroom came, and those who were ready went in with him to the wedding, and the door was shut.
Finally, the rest of the virgins also came, saying: O Lord, O Lord, open to us!
He answered and said: Truly I say, but I do not know you.
So stay awake, because you do not know the day or the hour when the Son of Man will come. The parable of the talents.
As if a traveling man called his servants and entrusted them with his money,
So he gave one five talents, another two talents, and another one talent. Each according to his ability. And he traveled at once.
So the one who took the five dinars went and traded with them, and gained another five dinars.
And so he who took the two weights also gained two other weights.
As for the one who took the balance, he went away and dug into the ground and hid his master's silver.
After a long time, the master of those slaves came and held them accountable.
Then came the one who had taken the five dinars and brought another five dinars, saying: O master, you gave me five dinars. Here are another five dinars I have earned on top of them.
So his master said to him: Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a little; I will set you over much. Enter into the joy of your master.
Then came the one who had taken the two weights and said: O master, you gave me two weights. Here are two other weights that I have gained besides them.
His master said to him: Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things; I will put you in charge of many things. Enter into the joy of your master.
Then came also the one who took the single coin and said: O master, I know that you are a harsh man, reaping where you did not sow, and gathering where you did not scatter seed.
I diminished and proceeded, and I concealed your weight on the earth. Behold, this is yours.
So his master answered him, saying: O wicked and lazy servant, did you know that I reap where I did not sow, and gather where I did not scatter?
So I should have deposited my silver with the money changers, for when I came, I would take what is mine with interest.
So take the weight from him and give it to the one who has ten weights.
For whoever has will be given more, and whoever does not have, even what they have will be taken from them.
And the worthless servant cast him into the outer darkness; there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. The sheep and the goats
And when the Son of Man comes in his glory, and all the angels with him, then he will sit on his glorious throne.
And all the peoples will be gathered before Him, and He will distinguish some of them from others just as a shepherd distinguishes the sheep from the goats,
And he will set the sheep on his right and the goats on his left.
Then the King will say to those on His right hand: Come, you who are blessed by my Father; inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world.
For I was hungry and you gave me food. I was thirsty and you gave me drink. I was a stranger and you took me in.
Naked, and you clothed me. Sick, and you visited me. Imprisoned, and you came to me.
Then the righteous will answer him, saying: "Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you, or thirsty and give you drink?"
And when did We see you a stranger and take you in, or naked and clothe you?
And when did we see you sick or in prison and come to you?
The king will answer them, "Truly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine, you did for me."
Then he also says to those on the left: Depart from me, you cursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels,
Because I was hungry, you did not feed me. I was thirsty, yet you did not give me drink.
I was a stranger, yet you did not shelter me. Naked, yet you did not clothe me. Sick and imprisoned, yet you did not visit me.
Then they also will answer him, saying: Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty or a stranger or naked or sick or in prison, and did not serve you?
He will answer them, saying: Truly, I say to you, as you did not do it to one of the least of these, you did not do it to me.
So these will go to eternal punishment, but the righteous to eternal life.
The conspiracy to kill Jesus
And when Jesus had finished all these sayings, he said to his disciples:
"You know that in two days it will be Passover, and the Son of Man will be handed over to be crucified."
Then the chief priests, the scribes, and the elders of the people gathered together in the house of the high priest, who was called Caiaphas,
And they conspired to seize Jesus by stealth and kill him.
But they said: "Not during the feast, lest there be a riot among the people." He poured the perfume on Jesus.
While Jesus was in Bethany at the house of Simon the leper,
A woman came to him with an alabaster jar of very expensive perfume, and she poured it on his head while he was reclining.
When his disciples saw this, they were indignant and said, "Why this waste?"
Because this perfume could have been sold for much more and given to the poor.
Then Jesus knew and said to them, "Why do you trouble the woman? For she has done a good work for me!"
For the poor you will always have with you, but you will not always have me.
For she poured this perfume on my body for the purpose of sufficing me.
Truly I tell you: wherever this gospel is preached throughout the world, what she has done will also be told in remembrance of her." Judas' betrayal
Then one of the twelve, called Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests
And he said, "What do you want to give me, and I will hand him over to you?" So they counted out thirty pieces of silver to him.
And from that time he sought an opportunity to hand him over. The Passover supper with the disciples
On the first day of Unleavened Bread, the disciples came to Jesus and asked him, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover?"
So he said: "Go to the city, to so-and-so, and tell him: The teacher says: My time is near. Prepare the Passover with my disciples at your place."
So the disciples did as Jesus commanded them and prepared the Passover.
And when evening came, he reclined with the twelve.
And while they were eating, he said: "Truly I tell you, one of you will betray me."
They were very saddened, and each one of them began to say to him: "Is it I, Lord?"
He answered and said: "The one who dips his hand with me in the dish is the one who will betray me!"
The Son of Man is going as it is written about him, but woe to that man by whom the Son of Man is betrayed! It would have been better for that man if he had not been born!
Then Judas, the one who betrayed him, answered and said, "Is it I, my lord?" He said to him, "You have said so." The Lord's Supper
While they were eating, Jesus took the bread, blessed it, broke it, and gave it to the disciples, saying: "Take, eat. This is my body."
He took the cup, gave thanks, and gave it to them, saying: "Drink from it, all of you,
For this is my blood of the new covenant, which is poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins.
And I tell you: From now on, I will not drink from the fruit of this vine until that day when I drink it new with you in the kingdom of my Father.
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus predicts Peter's denial of Him.
Then Jesus said to them, "You will all fall away on account of me this night, for it is written: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered.
But after I rise, I will go ahead of you to Galilee.
Then Peter answered him, "Even if everyone else doubts you, I will never doubt you."
Jesus said to him, "Truly I tell you, this very night, before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times."
Peter said to him, "Even if I have to die with you, I will never deny you!" All the disciples said the same. In Gethsemane
Then Jesus came with them to a place called Gethsemane, and said to the disciples, "Sit here while I go over there and pray."
Then he took Peter and the two sons of Zebedee with him, and began to be sorrowful and troubled.
He said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful, even to death. Stay here and keep watch with me."
Then he stepped forward a little and fell on his face, praying, saying: "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me, but not as I want, but as you want."
Then he came to the disciples and found them sleeping, and said to Peter, "So, could you not watch with me one hour?"
Stay awake and pray so that you do not enter into temptation. For the spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak.
Then he went away again and prayed, saying: "My Father, if it is not possible for this cup to pass from me unless I drink it, may your will be done."
Then he came and found them also sleeping, for their eyes were heavy.
So he left them and went on, and he prayed the third prayer saying those exact words.
Then he came to his disciples and said to them, "Sleep now and rest! Behold, the hour has come, and the Son of Man is delivered into the hands of sinners.
Let's go! Here is the one who is handing me over, he has approached!». The arrest of Jesus
And while he was speaking, Judas, one of the twelve, came with a great crowd armed with swords and clubs, sent from the chief priests and elders of the people.
And the one he handed over gave them a sign, saying: "The one I kiss is the one. Seize him."
Immediately he went up to Jesus and said, "Peace be with you, Lord!" and kissed him.
Then Jesus said to him, "Friend, why have you come?" At that moment they stepped forward, laid hands on Jesus, and seized him.
And one of those with Jesus reached out his hand and drew his sword and struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
Then Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back in its place, for all who take the sword will perish by the sword!"
Do you think I cannot now ask my father to provide me with more than twelve legions of angels?
So how do the books continue: Is it supposed to be like this?».
At that hour Jesus said to the crowds: "It is as if you came out with swords and clubs to arrest a thief! Every day I sat with you teaching in the temple, and you did not seize me.
And all this was to fulfill the prophets' writings." Then all the disciples left him and fled. Before the Jewish council
And those who seized Jesus led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and elders had gathered.
As for Peter, he followed from a distance to the house of the high priest, then went inside and sat among the servants to see the outcome.
And the chief priests and the elders and the whole council were seeking false testimony against Jesus in order to put him to death,
They did not find. And although many false witnesses came, they did not find. But finally, two false witnesses came forward.
And they said: "He said: I am able to destroy the temple of God, and in three days rebuild it."
Then the chief priests stood up and said to him, "Do you not answer anything? What is it that these men testify against you?"
And Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God to tell us: Are you the Christ, the Son of God?"
Jesus said to him, "You have said so! And I say to you: from now on you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of the power, and coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the chief priest tore his clothes and said, "He has blasphemed! What further need do we have of witnesses? Behold, you have now heard his blasphemy!"
What do you see?» They answered and said: «He deserves death.»
Then they spat in his face and struck him with their fists, and others slapped him.
Saying, "Prophesy to us, O Christ, who struck you?" Peter's denial
As for Peter, he was sitting outside in the courtyard, when a servant girl came to him and said, "You were with Jesus the Galilean!"
He denied in front of everyone, saying: "I do not know what you are saying!"
Then when he went out into the hallway, another saw him and said to those there, "This man was with Jesus the Nazarene!"
He also denied under oath: "I do not know the man!"
And after a little while, the bystanders came and said to Peter, "Surely you also are one of them, for your speech betrays you!"
Then he began to curse and swear: "I do not know the man!" And immediately the rooster crowed.
Then Peter remembered the word Jesus had said to him: "Before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times." And he went outside and wept bitterly.
Judas hangs himself.
And when morning came, all the chief priests and the elders of the people conferred together against Jesus to put him to death,
So they bound him and led him away and handed him over to Pilate the governor.
Then when Judas, who had betrayed him, saw that he was condemned, he regretted it and returned the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders.
He said: "I have erred in shedding innocent blood." They said: "What is our responsibility? You are the more knowledgeable!"
He threw the silver in the temple and left, then went away and strangled himself.
So the chief priests took the silver and said, "It is not lawful to put it into the treasury, because it is the price of blood."
So they consulted and bought with it the potter's field as a burial place for strangers.
That is why this field is called the "Field of Blood" to this day.
Then was fulfilled what was spoken by Jeremiah the prophet, saying: "And they took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of him who was valued, whom they of the children of Israel valued,"
And they gave her from the potter's field, as the Lord commanded me." Before Pilate
Then Jesus stood before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the King of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "You say so."
And while the chief priests and the elders were accusing him, he did not answer anything.
Then Pilate said to him, "Do you not hear how many testify against you?"
He did not answer him with a single word, which greatly astonished the governor.
The governor was accustomed during the festival to release one prisoner to the crowd, whoever they wanted.
At that time, they had a famous prisoner named Barabbas.
While they were gathered, Pilate said to them, "Whom do you want me to release for you? Barabbas or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Because he knew that they had handed him over out of envy.
And while he was sitting on the chair of authority, his wife sent to him saying: "Beware of that righteous one, for I suffered greatly today in a dream because of him."
But the chief priests and the elders stirred up the crowd to ask for Barabbas and to have Jesus executed.
The governor answered and said to them, "Which of the two do you want me to release for you?" They said, "Barabbas!"
Pilate said to them, "What shall I do with Jesus who is called the Christ?" They all said to him, "Let him be crucified!"
So the governor said: "And what evil deed?" They kept shouting louder, saying: "Crucify him!"
When Pilate saw that it was of no use, but rather that a riot was breaking out, he took water and washed his hands before the crowd, saying: "I am innocent of this righteous man's blood! See to it yourselves!"
Then all the people answered and said, "His blood be on us and on our children."
Then he released Barabbas for them; but Jesus he scourged and delivered to be crucified. The soldiers mocked him.
So the governor's soldiers took Jesus to the governor's headquarters and gathered the whole cohort against him,
They stripped him and dressed him in a scarlet robe,
And they put a crown of thorns on his head and a reed in his right hand. They knelt before him and mocked him, saying, "Hail, King of the Jews!"
They spat on him, took the cane, and hit him on the head.
And after they mocked him, they took off his robe and put his own clothes on him, and led him away to be crucified. The crucifixion
And as they were going out, they found a man from Kairouan named Simon, so they compelled him to carry his cross.
And when they came to a place called Golgotha, which is called "the place of the skull"
They gave him honey mixed with bitterness to drink. And when he tasted it, he did not want to drink.
And when they crucified him, they divided his clothes by casting lots for them, so that what was spoken by the prophet would be fulfilled: "They divided my clothes among them, and for my garment they cast lots."
Then they sat guarding him there.
And they put above his head the charge against him written: "This is Jesus, King of the Jews."
Then he was crucified with two thieves, one on the right and one on the left.
And the passersby were mocking him while shaking their heads.
saying, "You who destroy the temple and build it in three days, save yourself! If you are the Son of God, come down from the cross!"
And also the chief priests, mocking along with the scribes and elders, said:
Others were saved, but as for himself, he cannot save himself! If he is the King of Israel, let him come down now from the cross, and we will believe in him!
He may rely on God, so let Him save him now if He wills! For he said: I am the Son of God!».
And thus the two thieves who were crucified with him also mocked him. Death
And from the sixth hour there was darkness over all the land until the ninth hour.
And about the ninth hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying: "Eli, Eli, lema sabachthani?" that is: "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?"
So some of those who were standing there, when they heard it, said, "He is calling Elijah."
Immediately, one of them ran, took a sponge, filled it with vinegar, placed it on a reed, and gave it to him to drink.
As for the others, they said, "Leave him alone. Let's see if Elijah will come to save him!"
And Jesus cried out with a loud voice, and gave up the spirit.
And the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom. And the earth shook, and the rocks split,
And the graves were opened, and many bodies of the saints who had fallen asleep were raised.
And they came out of the tombs after His resurrection, entered the holy city, and appeared to many.
As for the centurion and those with him who were guarding Jesus, when they saw the earthquake and what had happened, they were very afraid and said, "Truly this was the Son of God!"
And there were many women looking from afar, who had followed Jesus from Galilee, serving him,
Among them were Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joses, and the mother of the sons of Zebedee. The burial
And when evening came, a rich man from Arama named Joseph came, and he was also a disciple of Jesus.
Then he went to Pilate and asked for the body of Jesus. So Pilate ordered that the body be given.
So Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth,
And he placed him in his new grave, which he had carved out of the rock, then rolled a large stone over the entrance of the grave and left.
And Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting opposite the tomb. The guards at the tomb
And on the next day, after the preparation, the chief priests and the Pharisees gathered together to Pilate.
Saying: "O Master, we remember that the deceiver said while he was alive: Indeed, after three days I will rise."
So he stayed guarding the tomb until the third day, lest his disciples come by night and steal him away, and tell the people: He has risen from the dead, and the last deception would be worse than the first!».
Then Pilate said to them, "You have guards. Go and secure him as you know how."
So they went and secured the grave with guards and sealed the stone.
The Resurrection
And after the Sabbath, at dawn on the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene and the other Mary came to look at the tomb.
And when a great earthquake occurred, because the angel of the Lord came down from heaven and came and rolled away the stone from the door, and sat on it.
And his appearance was like lightning, and his clothing white like snow.
Out of fear, the guards trembled and became like the dead.
The angel answered and said to the two women: "Do not be afraid, for I know that you seek Jesus who was crucified.
He is not here, for he has risen as he said! Come, see the place where the Lord was lying.
And go quickly and tell his disciples: He has risen from the dead. Behold, he goes before you into Galilee. There you will see him. Behold, I have told you.»
So they quickly came out of the tomb with great fear and joy, running to tell his disciples.
And as they were on their way to tell his disciples, Jesus met them and said, "Peace be with you." So they came to him, took hold of his feet, and worshiped him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me." The guards' statements
And while they were going, a group of guards came to the city and informed the chief priests of all that had happened.
So they gathered with the elders, consulted, and gave the soldiers much silver.
Saying: "Say that his disciples came by night and stole him while we were asleep."
And when the governor hears that, we appeal to him and make you reassured.
So they took the silver and did as they were taught, and this saying spread among the Jews to this day. Jesus appears to the disciples
As for the eleven disciples, they went to Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus had directed them.
And when they saw him, they prostrated to him, but some of them doubted.
Then Jesus came forward and said to them, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me,
Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
And teach them to observe all that I have commanded you. And behold, I am with you always, to the end of the age." Amen.
John the Baptist paves the way
The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God,
As it is written in the prophets: "Behold, I send my messenger before your face, who will prepare your way before you."
A voice crying out in the wilderness: Prepare the way of the Lord, make His paths straight.
John was baptizing in the wilderness and preaching a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins.
And all the region of Judea and the people of Jerusalem went out to him, and they were all baptized by him in the Jordan River, confessing their sins.
And John was clothed with camel's hair, with a leather belt around his waist, and he ate locusts and wild honey.
And he was preaching, saying: "After me comes one who is mightier than I, the one of whom I am not worthy to stoop down and untie the straps of his sandals."
"I baptized you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The baptism of Jesus Christ and His temptation
And in those days Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee and was baptized by John in the Jordan.
And immediately, while he was coming up out of the water, he saw the heavens torn open, and the Spirit descending like a dove upon him.
And there was a voice from the heavens: "You are my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
And immediately the Spirit drove him into the wilderness,
And he was in the wilderness for forty days, being tempted by the devil. He was with the wild beasts. And the angels were ministering to him. The calling of the first disciples
After John had been baptized, Jesus came to Galilee, preaching the good news of the kingdom of God.
And he says: "The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand; repent and believe in the Gospel."
And as he was walking by the Sea of Galilee, he saw Simon and his brother Andrew casting a net into the sea, for they were fishermen.
Then Jesus said to them, "Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
For the time being, they left their nets and followed him.
Then he went a little farther from there and saw Jacob son of Zebedee and John his brother, who were in the boat mending the nets.
So he called them for the time. They left their father Zubaydi on the ship with the servant and went after him. He expelled an impure spirit.
Then they entered Capernaum, and immediately on the Sabbath he entered the synagogue and began to teach.
They were astonished by his teaching because he taught them as one who had authority, and not like the scribes.
And there was in their assembly a man with an unclean spirit, and he cried out
Saying: "Ah! What have we to do with you, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are: the Holy One of God!"
Then Jesus rebuked him, saying, "Be silent! Come out of him!"
He struck down the impure spirit, cried out with a loud voice, and came out of him.
They were all amazed, until some of them asked one another, saying: "What is this? What is this new teaching? For he commands even unclean spirits, and they obey him!"
So his news immediately spread throughout all the surrounding region of Galilee. The healing of the mother-in-law of Simon and others.
And when they had left the synagogue, they went at once to the house of Simon and Andrew, with James and John,
And Samaan's mother-in-law was lying down feverish, so they immediately informed him about her.
He stepped forward and held her hand, so the fever immediately left her and she began to serve them.
And when evening came, as the sun set, all the sick and the insane came to him.
And the whole city was gathered at the door.
He healed many who were sick with various diseases, cast out many demons, and did not allow the demons to speak because they knew him. The preaching in Galilee
And very early in the morning he got up, went out, and went to a solitary place, and there he prayed,
Simon and those with him followed him.
And when they found him, they said to him, "Everyone is looking for you."
So he said to them: "Let us go to the neighboring villages, so that I may also preach there, for that is why I came out."
He was preaching in their synagogues throughout all Galilee and casting out demons. Healing of a leper
Then a leper came to him, kneeling and saying to him, "If you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus was moved with compassion, stretched out His hand, touched him, and said, "I am willing; be clean!"
While he was speaking, the leprosy left him and he was cleansed.
So he rebuked him and sent him away for the time being,
And he said to him, "Look, say nothing to anyone, but go, show yourself to the priest and offer the gift that Moses commanded for your cleansing, as a testimony to them."
As for him, he went out and began to call out loudly and spread the news, until he was no longer able to enter the city openly, but rather he stayed outside in deserted places, and people came to him from every direction.
Healing of a Paralyzed Person
Then he also entered Capernaum after a few days, and heard that he was in the house.
And soon many gathered until there was no more space, not even around the door. So he addressed them with a word.
They came to him bringing a paralyzed man carried by four.
And when they could not approach him because of the crowd, they uncovered the roof where he was. After they made a hole, they lowered the bed on which the paralyzed man was lying.
When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralyzed man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
And there were some of the scribes sitting there, thinking in their hearts:
"Why does this man speak like that, with blasphemies? Who can forgive sins except God alone?"
At that time, Jesus perceived in his spirit that they were thinking this way within themselves, and he said to them, "Why are you thinking this in your hearts?"
Which is easier, to say to the paralyzed: Your sins are forgiven, or to say: Get up, take your bed, and walk?
But so that you may know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." He said to the paralyzed man:
I tell you: Get up, take your bed, and go to your home!
He immediately got up, carried the bed, and went out in front of everyone, so that all were amazed and glorified God, saying, "We have never seen anything like this before!" The call of Levi
Then he also went out to the sea. And all the crowd came to him, and he taught them.
And while he was passing by, Levi son of Helfi saw him sitting at the place of tax collection, and said to him, "Follow me." So he got up and followed him.
And while he was reclining at his house, many tax collectors and sinners were reclining with Jesus and his disciples, because they were many and followed him.
As for the scribes and Pharisees, when they saw him eating with tax collectors and sinners, they said to his disciples, "Why does he eat and drink with tax collectors and sinners?"
When Jesus heard it, he said to them, "It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick. I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance." The question about fasting
Now the disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting, and they came and said to him, "Why do the disciples of John and the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not fast?"
Then Jesus said to them, "Can the children of the bridegroom fast while the bridegroom is with them? As long as the bridegroom is with them, they cannot fast.
But there will come days when the bridegroom is taken away from them, and then they will fast on those days.
No one sews a patch of new cloth onto an old garment, otherwise the new filling will take from the old and the tear will become worse.
And no one puts new wine into old wineskins, lest the new wine burst the wineskins; for the wine will be spilled, and the wineskins will be ruined. But they put new wine into new wineskins. Lord of the Sabbath
And he passed through the grainfields on the Sabbath; and his disciples began to pluck the ears of grain as they went along.
So the Pharisees said to him, "Look! Why are they doing what is not lawful on the Sabbath?"
He said to them: "Have you never read what David did when he was in need and hungry, he and those with him?
How he entered the house of God in the days of Abiathar the chief priest, and ate the consecrated bread which it is not lawful for anyone to eat except the priests, and also gave some to those who were with him.
Then he said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath."
If the Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath also.
Healing on Saturday
Then he also entered the synagogue, and there was a man whose hand was withered.
So they began to watch him closely to see if he would heal on the Sabbath, so that they might accuse him.
So he said to the man who had the dry hand: "Stand in the middle!"
Then he said to them: "Is it lawful to do good or to do evil on the Sabbath? To save a life or to kill?" They remained silent.
He looked around at them angrily, saddened by the hardness of their hearts, and said to the man: "Extend your hand." So he extended it, and his hand returned healthy like the other.
Then the Pharisees went out immediately with the Herodians and plotted against him to kill him. The crowds followed Jesus.
Then Jesus withdrew with his disciples to the sea, and a large crowd from Galilee and Judea followed him.
From Jerusalem, from Edom, and from beyond the Jordan, and those around Tyre and Sidon, a great multitude gathered, when they heard what he had done, they came to him.
He told his disciples to accompany him with a small boat for the sake of the crowd, so that they would not crowd him.
Because he had healed many, so everyone who had a disease would fall upon him to touch him.
And the impure spirits, when they saw him, fell down before him and cried out, saying: "You are the Son of God!"
And he strongly advised them not to reveal him. The selection of the twelve apostles
Then he ascended the mountain and called those he wanted, so they went to him.
And he appointed twelve to be with him, and to send them out to preach,
And they will have authority over healing diseases and casting out demons.
And He gave Simon the name Peter.
And Jacob son of Zebedee and John the brother of Jacob, and he gave them the name Boanerges, which means Sons of Thunder.
Andreas, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James son of Alphaeus, Thaddaeus, Simon the Zealot,
And Judas Iscariot, who betrayed him. Then they came to the house. Jesus and Beelzebul
So they gathered together so much that they could not even eat bread.
When his relatives heard, they went out to seize him, because they said, "He is insane!"
As for the scribes who came down from Jerusalem, they said: "He has Beelzebul with him! And it is by the prince of demons that he casts out demons."
He called them and spoke to them in parables: "How can a demon cast out a demon?"
And if a kingdom is divided against itself, that kingdom cannot stand.
And if a house is divided against itself, that house cannot stand.
